          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Rousseau's Philosophy</strong><br>\nRousseau's philosophy revolved around the idea that society corrupts natural goodness. He believed that humans were inherently good but became corrupted by the complex social structures and inequalities of civilization. His writings suggest a return to natural simplicity and freedom as remedies for social ills.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Social Contract</strong><br>\nOne of Rousseau's most famous works is \"The Social Contract,\" published in 1762. Here, he introduces the concept of the 'general will,' arguing for a form of government based on the collective will of the people. He believed that laws are binding only when supported by the people's common interest.</p>\n\n<p><strong>On Education: Emile</strong><br>\nIn \"Emile, or On Education,\" Rousseau outlines his philosophy on education. He proposes that education should foster rather than restrict children's natural instincts and that learning should be guided by experience rather than formal teaching. This work has had a lasting impact on educational theory.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
