          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Rousseau's Influence on the French Revolution</strong><br>\nRousseau's political ideas significantly influenced the French Revolution, which began shortly after his death. His views on liberty, equality, and the importance of the general will can be seen in the revolutionary slogan \"Liberty, Equality, Fraternity.\"</p>\n\n<p><strong>Later Life and Exile</strong><br>\nTowards the end of his life, Rousseau faced persecution from French and Swiss authorities because of his ideas. He was forced to live in exile in several locations, continuing to write and compose music. His health and personal relationships suffered during this period.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Legacy</strong><br>\nRousseau died on July 2, 1778, in Ermenonville, France. Despite living a life fraught with controversy and conflict, his ideas continued to resonate. They have influenced political thought, educational theory, and literature for centuries, making him one of the Enlightenment's most enduring figures.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
